T4 CI matrix round 4 — windows residual failures (gh run 42164382675,
windows-latest the only red leg). 2026-11-06.

== Failure 1: TestWriteDurableFileNeverTorn — SAME signature after
   round 3's replaceFile retry (PRODUCT, internal/mcp) ==

Routing verification first (per brief): grep os.Rename|replaceFile over
internal/mcp non-test files — writeDurableFile's rename is the ONLY
product rename and it routes through replaceFile (manifest.go:143).
No bypass. So the holder is the mechanism, and round 3's diagnosis was
half-right but the fix was on the wrong side:

  The test's 4 reader goroutines tight-loop os.ReadFile(driver.lua)
  (4MB per read). Go's opens carry FILE_SHARE_READ|WRITE but NOT
  FILE_SHARE_DELETE (deliberate, go issue #32031), so while ANY reader
  holds the file MoveFileEx cannot replace it. A tight-loop reader
  holds it ~99% of the time (close-to-reopen gap is sub-microsecond);
  the probability of ALL FOUR readers sitting in that gap
  simultaneously is effectively nil - a 2s writer-side retry window can
  never land. The secondary "TempDir cleanup: unlinkat driver.lua:
  being used by another process" is downstream: the early t.Fatalf
  leaves the reader goroutines running into the framework's RemoveAll.

Fix (the READ side): readFileShared -
  internal/mcp/readshared_unix.go     //go:build !windows  (os.ReadFile)
  internal/mcp/readshared_windows.go  CreateFileW GENERIC_READ with
    FILE_SHARE_READ|WRITE|DELETE (x/sys/windows, already a direct dep);
    ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND/PATH_NOT_FOUND map to fs.ErrNotExist so
    os.IsNotExist semantics are unchanged.
  Readers swapped: loadManifest (manifest.go), ws_get + driver-push
  read-back (workspace.go x2), and the never-torn hammer's reader loop
  (workspace_test.go) - the contract reader never blocks the atomic
  replace; replaceFile's retry stays as the belt for non-reader holders
  (a scanner). rename_windows.go comment updated with the round-4
  lesson.

== Failure 2: claim lock open "Access is denied" fatal (PRODUCT,
   internal/server sockclaim_windows.go) ==

Round 13 shape: "claim transition lock race.sock.lck: open ...: Access
is denied" -> bare loser (winners=1 graceful=4 bare=1). Windows returns
ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED (5) - not EEXIST - when the previous holder's lock
file is concurrently being DELETED (close-handle to delete window), and
ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION (32) for a handle mid-close: both are
CONTENTION. Round 3's claim loop treated every non-EEXIST error as
fatal.

Fix: claim-open errors classified - EEXIST, errno 5, errno 32 all mean
"contended" and fall into the SAME bounded wait (500ms grace poll,
lock-gone -> retry claim, sock alive -> graceful loser, stale recovery
unchanged); anything else stays fatal. Constants named accessDenied /
sharingViolation at the top of the file with the deletion-window
explanation.

